////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// v0.9.0

Version 0.9 brings new data modes, edit coalescing, a new digit cursor
mode, various fixes and quality of life changes.

// PieceV3 & Coalescing

The new document editor revision, PieceV3, now has support for coalescing
edits.

Instead of undoing every single edit one-by-one, if a series of edits were
made next to each other, the series of edits are undone.

This is akin to modern text editors, where a word can be written, and a
single undo removes the word.

To disable this feature, set configuration 'coalesce' to "off". It only
affects future edits.

// Data Modes

Data modes have been added: "x16", "x32", "d8", "d16", "d32", "o8", "o16",
and "o32".

These provide new ways to represent data, and you can still directly input
existing and new elements into the document.

Example using "o32" and octal addresses (4 columns):

    oct            0           4          10          14
      0  04010020040 04010020040 04010020040 04010020040
     20  04010020040 04044313342 04010020040 22670420040      ...       ..
     40  22145561221 04044313342 04010020040 22670420040  .......       ..
     60  22145561221 04044313342 22145561040 34244313342  .......  .......
  ...
  OVR | o32 |    ascii |        0

For example, I managed to write an octal-encoded message from a screenshot
to see its ASCII text data.

Set by the 'data' configuration field, or via the command-line with
'--data=MODE'.

// Digit Cursor

A new digit cursor mode was added, next to insert and overwrite.

This enables editing a single digit (or nibble), avoids having to retype
the entire element.

The new cycle is: Overwrite -> Insert -> Digit -> Overwrite.

For example, if you have your cursor on the lower nibble of F0, and type
'f', it will register as FF, versus having to type "FF", or worse under
x32: FFFFFFFF.

// Improved File Handling

In 0.8.3, when a file couldn't be opened as read-write, it would be
confusing what other options were available, even if it would succeed with
'-R'.

In 0.9, all files are opened as read-only. If an operating system error
occurs, other than "file not found" (for a new buffer), it is reported
(i.e., permission issue).

There are other fixes to make it possible for ddhx to save in network
shares (tested GVFS with an SMB share, where O_RDWR and chmod.2 are
disallowed).

// Fast Save Optimization

When the operating system allows a file to be opened as read-write, only
the dirty regions are written onto the file.

Otherwise, the fallback to traditional saving method is used (write and
replace).

No more writing the entire 100 GiB file if a single byte was changed!

// Colors

ddhx 0.9 now has support for color mappings with the 'color' configuration
and command.

For example, to set a different background color to the cursor:
'color cursor :blue' (blue background)

Here are available elements:
- 'normal': The default.
- 'cursor': The cursor.
- 'selection': The active selection.
- 'mirror': The mirrored cursor.
- 'zero': When data value is zero (affected by the 'highlight-zeros'
  config).

Syntax: "foreground:background". Both are optional, empty meaning terminal
default.

Colors:
- 'default': Default terminal color.
- 'invert': Invert color from default terminal color.
- Colors: black, blue, green, aqua, red, purple, yellow, gray, lightgray,
  brightblue, brightgreen, brightaqua, brightred, brightpurple,
  brightyellow, white.

// Added
- "highlight-zeros" configuration.
- "x16", "x32", "d8", "d16", "d32", "o8", "o16", "o32" data modes.
- Digit cursor mode.
- Input coalescing with "piecev3" backend.
- Support for color schemes.

// Changed
- Fast Save Optimization.
- Fallback to traditional saving method to help with network shares.
- Improve handling files (properly error out on permission errors).
- If opened as restricted, append "[readonly]" into the initial message.
- 'address-spacing' now accepts negative values for right-alignment.
- '--readonly' was renamed to '--restrict' to better reflect its function.
  '-R' stays the same.
- Renamed confusing "addressing" configuration to "address", to be
  consistent with "data".

// Fixed
- "save-as" command not working in read-only/restricted mode.
- Fixed opening and editing an empty file.
- Fixed '--addressing-spacing' calling charset config instead.
- On Windows, using conhost, re-hide the cursor on console resize.
- Fixed Backspace and Ctrl+Backspace being inverted on FreeBSD.

// Removed
- The "autoresize" configuration is removed. Use "columns auto" instead,
  or Alt+R within the editor.

// Docs
- Command descriptions were revised for clarity.
- New ddhx.1 manpage.

